TicketHub — Design Document
1. Context
TicketHub is a website that sells tickets for concerts and events. Promoters create events with a seating layout and a price per section; customers browse events, pick specific seats, hold them briefly while paying, and receive a ticket they can show at the door. The hard part is not selling a ticket — it is selling the same seat to exactly one person, even when a very popular concert goes on sale and tens of thousands of people hit "Buy" at the same second.

2. Goals and Non-goals
Goals

A seat can be sold to at most one customer, ever. No double-booking, under any load.

Browsing stays fast and available even during a big sale.

The purchase flow is fair: a seat is held for a short, fixed window while the buyer pays, then released if payment fails or times out.

Customers can always view the tickets they own.

Non-goals

No resale/marketplace between customers (v1).

No dynamic pricing or auctions.

No mobile app — responsive web only.

No refunds workflow beyond cancelling an unpaid hold.

3. Requirements and Estimates
Functional requirements
Browse a list of events and view an event's details.

View the seat map for an event, with each seat's status (available / held / sold).

Hold one or more seats for a limited time (e.g. 10 minutes) while paying.

Pay for held seats and receive a ticket.

View "my tickets".

Promoters can create an event with a seat layout (admin path).

Non-functional requirements
Correctness: zero double-bookings. This is the top priority — a wrong sale is worse than a slow one.

Speed: browsing and seat-map reads under 300 ms; the hold and pay actions under 1 s.

Fairness: no single user or bot can grab a whole section; holds expire so abandoned carts release seats.

Availability: browsing and seat maps ~99.9%; the checkout path may degrade gracefully (queue) rather than fail.

Durability: a paid ticket is never lost.

Estimates
Normal traffic

50,000 events, ~500 seats each → ~25M seats total.

200,000 daily active users; ~1M event/seat-map reads per day ≈ 12 reads/s average.

~20,000 tickets sold per day ≈ 0.25 writes/s.

Read-heavy, small and comfortable for one database plus a cache.

Big-sale peak

A stadium concert: 60,000 seats, 500,000 people trying at once when sales open.

Traffic spike of ~20,000–50,000 requests/s for the first few minutes — roughly 1,000× normal.

Writes spike too: thousands of hold attempts per second, though only 60,000 can ever succeed.

The gap between normal (~12 reads/s) and peak (~50,000 req/s) is the whole design problem: the system must be cheap at rest and survive a short, extreme burst without ever breaking correctness.

4. Proposed Design
4.1 API
Method	Path	Purpose
GET	/events	List/search events (?q=, ?page=) → 200 with event summaries.
GET	/events/{id}	Event details + section prices → 200, or 404.
GET	/events/{id}/seats	Seat map with status per seat → 200. Served from cache where possible.
POST	/events/{id}/holds	Body {"seatIds": ["A12","A13"]}. Atomically holds seats for 10 min → 201 with {"holdId", "expiresAt"}, or 409 Conflict if any seat is unavailable.
POST	/holds/{holdId}/pay	Body {"paymentToken": "..."}. Converts the hold into an order → 201 with {"orderId", "ticketIds"}, 402 on payment failure, 410 if the hold expired.
DELETE	/holds/{holdId}	Release a hold early → 204.
GET	/me/tickets	The caller's tickets → 200.
POST	/admin/events	Promoter creates an event + seat layout → 201.
Status codes are used honestly: 409 for a lost seat race, 410 for an expired hold, 402 for a declined payment, 404 for unknown resources.

4.2 Data model
Four core tables (plus a holds table that makes correctness explicit):

sql
CREATE TABLE users (
  id          BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  email       TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  name        TEXT NOT NULL,
  created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE TABLE events (
  id          BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  name        TEXT NOT NULL,
  venue       TEXT NOT NULL,
  starts_at   TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
  sale_opens_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
  created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE TABLE seats (
  id          BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  event_id    BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES events(id),
  section     TEXT NOT NULL,
  row_label   TEXT NOT NULL,
  seat_number TEXT NOT NULL,
  price_cents INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (price_cents >= 0),
  status      TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'available'
              CHECK (status IN ('available','held','sold')),
  UNIQUE (event_id, section, row_label, seat_number)
);

CREATE TABLE orders (
  id          BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  user_id     BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id),
  event_id    BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES events(id),
  total_cents INTEGER NOT NULL,
  status      TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'paid'
              CHECK (status IN ('pending','paid','cancelled')),
  created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE TABLE order_items (
  order_id BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES orders(id),
  seat_id  BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES seats(id),
  PRIMARY KEY (order_id, seat_id),
  UNIQUE (seat_id)          -- a seat can appear in at most one order
);

CREATE TABLE holds (
  id         UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
  user_id    BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id),
  seat_id    BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES seats(id),
  expires_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
  UNIQUE (seat_id)          -- a seat can be held by at most one hold
);
Relationships: a user has many orders; an event has many seats; an order has many order_items, each pointing at exactly one seat. The UNIQUE (seat_id) constraints on order_items and holds are the database-level guarantee that a seat is never sold or held twice.

4.3 Preventing double-booking (the core requirement)
Double-booking is prevented at three layers, so that no single bug or race can cause it:

A single atomic hold operation. Holding seats runs inside one database transaction:

sql
BEGIN;
-- lock the rows we want to hold, skipping any already held/sold
SELECT id, status FROM seats
 WHERE id = ANY($seatIds) AND status = 'available'
 FOR UPDATE;
-- only proceed if we locked every requested seat
INSERT INTO holds (user_id, seat_id, expires_at) VALUES (...);
UPDATE seats SET status = 'held' WHERE id = ANY($seatIds);
COMMIT;
SELECT ... FOR UPDATE takes row locks, so two concurrent requests for the same seat serialize: the first wins, the second sees status <> 'available' and returns 409. The UNIQUE (seat_id) on holds is a second line of defence.

A database constraint as the final backstop. Even if application code were buggy, UNIQUE (seat_id) on order_items means the second insert for a seat fails at the database level. Constraints are the one thing you cannot forget to call.

Expiring holds release seats safely. A background job (or a scheduled query) finds holds past expires_at, deletes them, and flips the seats back to available — all in one transaction, so a seat is never released while a payment is mid-flight. Payment checks the hold is still valid (expires_at > now()) inside the same transaction that creates the order.

The result: two people can try to buy the same seat, but exactly one transaction commits, and the other gets a clear 409.

4.4 Architecture
text
                 ┌──────────────┐
   users ──────▶ │ Load Balancer│
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        │
          ┌─────────────┼──────────────┐
          ▼             ▼              ▼
   ┌───────────┐  ┌───────────┐  ┌────────────┐
   │ App server│  │ App server│  │ App server │   (stateless, autoscaled)
   └─────┬─────┘  └─────┬─────┘  └─────┬──────┘
         │              │              │
         ├──────▶ Redis (seat-map cache, rate limits, queue counters)
         │
         ├──────▶ PostgreSQL primary  ◀── writes: holds, orders, payments
         │              │
         │              ▼
         │        Read replicas  ◀── reads: event lists, seat maps on cache miss
         │
         └──────▶ Payment provider (external)

   Background worker: expires holds, releases seats, sends tickets
Each component justified by a problem:

Load balancer + stateless app servers: one server cannot take 50,000 req/s and would be a single point of failure. Stateless servers mean any request can go to any server, and we can autoscale during a sale.

Redis cache: seat maps and event lists are read constantly and change slowly. Caching them absorbs the browsing storm so the database only handles real state changes.

PostgreSQL primary: the source of truth for holds and orders. Row locks and unique constraints live here — this is where correctness is enforced.

Read replicas: serve browsing reads, and can take over if the primary fails.

Background worker: expires abandoned holds so seats return to sale without a customer action.

Payment provider: external, because handling card data ourselves is a compliance and security burden we deliberately avoid.

Surviving the big sale: the sale opens with a burst of reads (cache absorbs it), then a burst of hold attempts. App servers are autoscaled ahead of the sale; the hold transaction is short and locks only the requested seat rows, so contention is per-seat, not global. A queue/token-bucket at the load balancer smooths the first seconds so the primary is not overwhelmed, and per-user rate limits stop bots from holding entire sections. If the primary is saturated, reads still succeed from replicas and cache; only holds queue briefly.

5. Trade-offs
Pessimistic row locking vs. optimistic versioning. I chose SELECT ... FOR UPDATE (pessimistic). It is simple and guarantees correctness, but under extreme contention many transactions wait on the same rows. The alternative — optimistic concurrency with a version column and retries — has less locking overhead but pushes retry logic into the client and is harder to reason about. For a correctness-critical purchase, I accepted the simpler, safer option.

Short hold window (10 min) vs. customer convenience. Shorter holds release abandoned carts fast, which is fairer during a big sale but can frustrate a slow buyer who loses their seat mid-payment. Longer holds are friendlier but let a few users park seats and starve others. I chose 10 minutes as a balance, and the design makes the window a single config value so it can be tuned per event.

Serving seat maps from cache vs. perfect freshness. Cached seat maps can show a seat as available a second after it was held, so a user may occasionally see a 409 on hold. That is acceptable because the hold is the authoritative check; showing slightly stale data is far cheaper than querying the primary for every page view.

6. Risks and Open Questions
Payment provider outage during a sale: holds may expire while payment is retried. Open question: do we extend holds if the provider is down (fairness vs. complexity)?

Bot traffic: rate limits help, but sophisticated bots may need CAPTCHA or a waiting-room queue. Undecided for v1.

Hot events: one event's seats are a hot partition. If contention proves too high, shard seats by section — but that complicates cross-section holds.

Clock skew on expires_at between app servers: mitigate by using database time (now()) for all expiry checks, not server time.